        Verse(chapter: 2, verse: "20",
              sanskrit: #"न जायते म्रियते वा कदाचिन्नायं भूत्वा भविता वा न भूयः ।\#nअजो नित्यः शाश्वतोऽयं पुराणो न हन्यते हन्यमाने शरीरे ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Arjuna's deepest fear is that by fighting he will destroy the people he loves. Krishna answers with one of the Gita's central teachings: the Self (atman) is never born and never dies; it is not killed when the body is killed. This verse is the heart of his argument that death is not the end it appears to be. The lesson is that who we truly are is not limited to the body, which can loosen the grip of fear and grief."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"This is never born, nor does It die. It is not that not having been It again comes into being. (Or according to another view: It is not that having been It again ceases to be). This is unborn, eternal, changeless, ever-Itself. It is not killed when the body is killed."#,
