              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 2, verse: "62–63",
              sanskrit: #"ध्यायतो विषयान्पुंसः सङ्गस्तेषूपजायते ।\#nसङ्गात्सञ्जायते कामः कामात्क्रोधोऽभिजायते ॥\#nक्रोधाद्भवति सम्मोहः सम्मोहात्स्मृतिविभ्रमः ।\#nस्मृतिभ्रंशाद्बुद्धिनाशो बुद्धिनाशात्प्रणश्यति ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Continuing his portrait of the steady-minded person, Krishna explains how people lose their balance. It starts small: dwelling on something creates attachment, attachment becomes desire, frustrated desire becomes anger, anger clouds judgement, memory and reason fail, and the person is ruined. This chain is one of the Gita's sharpest pieces of psychology. The lesson is to catch the process early, at the level of what we let our minds dwell on."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Thinking of objects, attachment to them is formed in a man. From attachment longing, and from longing anger grows. From anger comes delusion, and from delusion loss of memory. From loss of memory comes the ruin of discrimination, and from the ruin of discrimination he perishes."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Thinking of objects, attachment to them is formed … From anger comes delusion … he perishes."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 2, verse: "64",
              sanskrit: #"रागद्वेषवियुक्तैस्तु विषयानिन्द्रियैश्चरन् ।\#nआत्मवश्यैर्विधेयात्मा प्रसादमधिगच्छति ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"After describing how desire leads to anger and ruin, Krishna gives the positive picture. The disciplined person still moves among the things of the world, but with the senses under control and without being pushed by liking or disliking. Such a person reaches a deep tranquillity. The lesson is that peace does not require running from life; it comes from engaging with it without being ruled by our cravings."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"But the self-controlled man, moving among objects with senses under restraint, and free from attraction and aversion, attains to tranquillity."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"The self-controlled man … free from attraction and aversion, attains to tranquillity."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 2, verse: "66",
              sanskrit: #"नास्ति बुद्धिरयुक्तस्य न चायुक्तस्य भावना ।\#nन चाभावयतः शान्तिरशान्तस्य कुतः सुखम् ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna traces a chain. Without inner steadiness there is no real understanding and no meditation; without meditation there is no peace; and without peace, how can there be happiness? Each link depends on the one before. The lesson is that happiness is not a separate goal we can grab directly; it grows out of a steady mind and a peaceful heart."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"No knowledge (of the Self) has the unsteady. Nor has he meditation. To the unmeditative there is no peace. And how can one without peace have happiness?"#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"To the unmeditative there is no peace. And how can one without peace have happiness?"#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 2, verse: "67",
              sanskrit: #"इन्द्रियाणां हि चरतां यन्मनोऽनुविधीयते ।\#nतदस्य हरति प्रज्ञां वायुर्नावमिवाम्भसि ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna compares the mind to a boat on the water. If it follows the wandering senses, it is swept along like a boat blown off course by the wind, and good judgment goes with it. The image is vivid and practical. The lesson is to guard where our attention goes, because whatever the mind follows will steer the whole of us."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"For, the mind which follows in the wake of the wandering senses, carries away his discrimination, as a wind (carries away from its course) a boat on the waters."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"The mind which follows in the wake of the wandering senses, carries away his discrimination."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 2, verse: "69",
              sanskrit: #"या निशा सर्वभूतानां तस्यां जागर्ति संयमी ।\#nयस्यां जाग्रति भूतानि सा निशा पश्यतो मुनेः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"In this striking verse, Krishna says that what is night for ordinary beings is day for the self-controlled sage, and what ordinary beings are awake to is night for the sage. The things most people chase hold little interest for the wise, while the inner life most people overlook is where the wise are fully awake. The lesson is that wisdom often means valuing what the world ignores."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"That which is night to all beings, in that the self-controlled man wakes. That in which all beings wake, is night to the Self-seeing Muni."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"That which is night to all beings, in that the self-controlled man wakes."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 2, verse: "70",
              sanskrit: #"आपूर्यमाणमचलप्रतिष्ठं समुद्रमापः प्रविशन्ति यद्वत् ।\#nतद्वत्कामा यं प्रविशन्ति सर्वे स शान्तिमाप्नोति न कामकामी ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"This verse comes at the end of Chapter 2, closing Krishna's description of the person of steady wisdom. He compares such a person to the ocean: rivers pour into it constantly, yet it stays full and undisturbed. Desires still arrive, but they don't overflow or toss the wise person around. The lesson is that peace doesn't come from chasing every desire, but from being deep enough to let them come and go."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"As into the ocean,—brimful, and still,—flow the waters, even so the Muni into whom enter all desires, he, and not the desirer of desires, attains to peace."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"As into the ocean … flow the waters, even so the Muni … attains to peace."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 2, verse: "71",
              sanskrit: #"विहाय कामान्यः सर्वान्पुमांश्चरति निःस्पृहः ।\#nनिर्ममो निरहंकारः स शांतिमधिगच्छति ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna closes his description of the person of steady wisdom: someone who has let go of desires, lives without craving, and is free of the sense of 'I' and 'mine'. Such a person finds peace. The lesson is that much of our restlessness comes from wanting and possessing, and that loosening our grip on 'me' and 'mine' opens the way to calm."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"That man who lives devoid of longing, abandoning all desires, without the sense of 'I' and 'mine,' he attains to peace."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"That man who lives devoid of longing, abandoning all desires … he attains to peace."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 3, verse: "4",
              sanskrit: #"न कर्मणामनारम्भान्नैष्कर्म्यं पुरुषोऽश्नुते ।\#nन च संन्यसनादेव सिद्धिं समधिगच्छति ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Arjuna has asked why he should act at all if knowledge is higher than action. Krishna replies that simply not working does not free anyone from the effects of work, and merely giving things up does not bring perfection. Running away from duty is not the same as rising above it. The lesson is that freedom comes from changing how we act, not from refusing to act."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"By non-performance of work none reaches worklessness; by merely giving up action no one attains to perfection."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"By merely giving up action no one attains to perfection."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 3, verse: "5",
              sanskrit: #"न हि कश्चित्क्षणमपि जातु तिष्ठत्यकर्मकृत् ।\#nकार्यते ह्यवशः कर्म सर्वः प्रकृतिजैर्गुणैः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna points out that no one can stay without acting even for a moment. Breathing, thinking and moving are all action, and our very nature keeps us active. So the real question is not whether to act but how. The lesson is that since we will always be acting anyway, we may as well act well, with care and without selfish attachment."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Verily none can ever rest for even an instant, without performing action; for all are made to act, helplessly indeed, by the Gunas, born of Prakriti."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Verily none can ever rest for even an instant, without performing action."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 3, verse: "8",
              sanskrit: #"नियतं कुरु कर्म त्वं कर्म ज्यायो ह्यकर्मणः ।\#nशरीरयात्राऽपि च ते न प्रसिध्येदकर्मणः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"At the start of Chapter 3, a confused Arjuna asks why, if wisdom is superior to action, Krishna is urging him into a terrible battle. Krishna replies that no one can truly stop acting, and that doing one's duty is better than inaction; even keeping the body alive requires action. Withdrawing from life is not the same as being spiritual. The lesson is to engage with your responsibilities rather than hide from them."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Do thou perform obligatory action; for action is superior to inaction, and even the bare maintenance of thy body would not be possible if thou art inactive."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Do thou perform obligatory action; for action is superior to inaction."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 3, verse: "19",
              sanskrit: #"तस्मादसक्तः सततं कार्यं कर्म समाचर ।\#nअसक्तो ह्याचरन्कर्म परमाप्नोति पूरुषः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna is still answering Arjuna's question about why he should act at all. His conclusion is to always do the work that needs doing, but without attachment, because that is how a person reaches the highest goal. He goes on to mention King Janaka, who reached perfection through action rather than by renouncing the world. The lesson is that the path to freedom runs through our everyday duties, done selflessly."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Therefore, do thou always perform actions which are obligatory, without attachment;—by performing action without attachment, one attains to the highest."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"By performing action without attachment, one attains to the highest."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 3, verse: "21",
              sanskrit: #"यद्यदाचरति श्रेष्ठस्तत्तदेवेतरो जनः ।\#nस यत्प्रमाणं कुरुते लोकस्तदनुवर्तते ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna is explaining why even a wise person should keep working: for the welfare and holding together of the world. Whatever a great person does, others copy; whatever standard they set, the world follows. Right after this, he points to himself as an example: he has nothing to gain, yet he keeps acting so people are not led astray. For Arjuna, a famous prince and warrior, walking away would send a message to everyone watching. The lesson is that those with influence carry extra responsibility, because people follow what they do, not only what they say."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Whatsoever the superior person does, that is followed by others. What he demonstrates by action, that, people follow."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Whatsoever the superior person does, that is followed by others."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 3, verse: "27",
              sanskrit: #"प्रकृतेः क्रियमाणानि गुणैः कर्माणि सर्वशः ।\#nअहङ्कारविमूढात्मा कर्ताऽहमिति मन्यते ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna is explaining why action doesn't have to bind a person. All actions are carried out by nature (prakriti) through its three qualities, the gunas, but the ego claims, 'I am the doer.' In the next verse he says the wise see 'qualities acting on qualities' and so stay unattached. This doesn't remove responsibility; it shows that suffering comes from the ego's false claim of ownership. The lesson is to act fully while loosening the ego's grip on credit and blame."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"The Gunas of Prakriti perform all action. With the understanding deluded by egoism, man thinks, “I am the doer.”"#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"The Gunas of Prakriti perform all action … man thinks, “I am the doer.”"#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 3, verse: "33",
              sanskrit: #"सदृशं चेष्टते स्वस्याः प्रकृतेर्ज्ञानवानपि ।\#nप्रकृतिं यान्ति भूतानि निग्रहः किं करिष्यति ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna observes that even the wise act according to their own nature; all beings follow their nature, so what can mere repression achieve? He is not saying we are helpless, but that forcing ourselves against our grain rarely works. The lesson is to understand our own nature and work with it wisely, instead of simply trying to suppress it."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Even a wise man acts in accordance with his own nature: beings follow nature: what can restraint do?"#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Even a wise man acts in accordance with his own nature: beings follow nature."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 3, verse: "34",
              sanskrit: #"इन्द्रियस्येन्द्रियस्यार्थे रागद्वेषौ व्यवस्थितौ ।\#nतयोर्न वशमागच्छेत्तौ ह्यस्य परिपन्थिनौ ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Right after saying that beings follow their nature, Krishna adds a warning. Attraction and aversion towards the things of the senses arise naturally, but we must not come under their control, because they are our enemies on the path. The lesson is that likes and dislikes will always appear, and our freedom lies in not letting them make our choices for us."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Attachment and aversion of the senses for their respective objects are natural: let none come under their sway: they are his foes."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Attachment and aversion … are natural: let none come under their sway: they are his foes."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 3, verse: "35",
              sanskrit: #"श्रेयान्स्वधर्मो विगुणः परधर्मात्स्वनुष्ठितात् ।\#nस्वधर्मे निधनं श्रेयः परधर्मो भयावहः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Near the end of Chapter 3, Krishna warns Arjuna against abandoning his own path for someone else's. Arjuna is a warrior, yet he has been suggesting he would be better off living as a beggar-monk. Krishna says it is better to follow your own duty imperfectly than another's perfectly, and even to die in your own duty. The lesson is authenticity: live the life that is truly yours, rather than imitating a path that only looks better from the outside."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Better is one's own Dharma, (though) imperfect, than the Dharma of another well-performed. Better is death in one's own Dharma: the Dharma of another is fraught with fear."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Better is one's own Dharma, (though) imperfect, than the Dharma of another well-performed."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 3, verse: "37",
              sanskrit: #"काम एष क्रोध एष रजोगुणसमुद्भवः ।\#nमहाशनो महापाप्मा विद्ध्येनमिह वैरिणम् ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Arjuna asks what drives a person to do wrong, almost against their will. Krishna answers plainly: it is desire, which turns into anger when it is blocked, and it is born of restless passion. This, he says, is the great enemy here. The lesson is to recognise desire and anger as the real forces behind many of our mistakes, so we can guard against them."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"It is desire—it is anger, born of the Rajo-guna: of great craving, and of great sin; know this as the foe here (in this world)."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"It is desire—it is anger … know this as the foe here (in this world)."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 3, verse: "39",
              sanskrit: #"आवृतं ज्ञानमेतेन ज्ञानिनो नित्यवैरिणा ।\#nकामरूपेण कौन्तेय दुष्पूरेणानलेन च ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna describes desire as the constant enemy of the wise, an unappeasable fire that covers true knowledge as smoke covers a flame. However much we feed it, it is never satisfied. The lesson is that endless wanting does not just make us restless; it hides our own clear understanding from us."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Knowledge is covered by this, the constant foe of the wise, O son of Kunti, the unappeasable fire of desire."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Knowledge is covered by this … the unappeasable fire of desire."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 3, verse: "42",
              sanskrit: #"इन्द्रियाणि पराण्याहुरिन्द्रियेभ्यः परं मनः ।\#nमनसस्तु परा बुद्धिर्यो बुद्धेः परतस्तु सः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna sets out a ladder of the inner life: the senses are higher than the body, the mind is higher than the senses, the intellect is higher than the mind, and higher than the intellect is the Self. In the next verse he tells Arjuna to use this knowledge to conquer desire. The lesson is that we are more than our impulses, and the higher parts of us can guide the lower."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"The senses are said to be superior (to the body); the mind is superior to the senses; the intellect is superior to the mind; and that which is superior to the intellect is He (the Atman)."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"The mind is superior to the senses … that which is superior to the intellect is He (the Atman)."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 4, verse: "7",
              sanskrit: #"यदा यदा हि धर्मस्य ग्लानिर्भवति भारत ।\#nअभ्युत्थानमधर्मस्य तदाऽऽत्मानं सृजाम्यहम् ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"In Chapter 4, Krishna reveals that he taught this same yoga to the sun god long ago. Arjuna is puzzled, since Krishna was born recently, so Krishna explains that he has had many births. Whenever righteousness (dharma) declines and wrongdoing rises, he brings himself forth into the world. This is the Gita's key statement of the idea of avatar, the divine descending into the world. The lesson is hope: in the darkest times, goodness finds a way to return."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Whenever, O descendant of Bharata, there is decline of Dharma, and rise of Adharma, then I body Myself forth."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Whenever … there is decline of Dharma, and rise of Adharma, then I body Myself forth."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 4, verse: "8",
              sanskrit: #"परित्राणाय साधूनां विनाशाय च दुष्कृताम् ।\#nधर्मसंस्थापनार्थाय सम्भवामि युगे युगे ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"This verse completes Krishna's explanation, begun in the previous one, of why he takes birth in the world. He comes to protect the good, to end wrongdoing, and to re-establish righteousness, 'age after age'. Spoken on a battlefield where a righteous war is about to begin, it also frames Arjuna's own role in restoring order. The lesson is that the work of protecting what is good never ends, and each of us can take part in it."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"For the protection of the good, for the destruction of the wicked, and for the establishment of Dharma, I come into being in every age."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"For the protection of the good … I come into being in every age."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 4, verse: "11",
              sanskrit: #"ये यथा मां प्रपद्यन्ते तांस्तथैव भजाम्यहम् ।\#nमम वर्त्मानुवर्तन्ते मनुष्याः पार्थ सर्वशः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna tells Arjuna that however people approach him, he responds to them in the same way, and that all people, whatever path they take, are in the end following his path. It is a generous and inclusive teaching. The lesson is that the divine meets each person where they are, and that sincere seekers on many different roads are moving towards the same goal."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"In whatever way men worship Me, in the same way do I fulfil their desires: (it is) My path, O son of Prithâ, (that) men tread, in all ways."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"In whatever way men worship Me, in the same way do I fulfil their desires."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 4, verse: "18",
              sanskrit: #"कर्मण्यकर्म यः पश्येदकर्मणि च कर्म यः ।\#nस बुद्धिमान् मनुष्येषु स युक्तः कृत्स्नकर्मकृत् ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna offers a riddle: the wise see inaction in action and action in inaction. Outer busyness can be inwardly empty, while true stillness can be full of purpose. What matters is the state of mind behind what we do. The lesson is that real action is measured by its inner quality, not by how much movement we see on the surface."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"He who sees inaction in action, and action in inaction, he is intelligent among men, he is a Yogi and a doer of all action."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"He who sees inaction in action, and action in inaction, he is intelligent among men."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 4, verse: "20",
              sanskrit: #"त्यक्त्वा कर्मफलासङ्गं नित्यतृप्तो निराश्रयः ।\#nकर्मण्यभिप्रवृत्तोऽपि नैव किञ्चित्करोति सः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna describes someone who has let go of clinging to the results of their work. They are always content and depend on nothing outside themselves, so even while fully engaged in activity, they are not bound by it. The lesson is that it is attachment, not activity, that ties us down, and that we can work hard while remaining inwardly free."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Forsaking the clinging to fruits of action, ever satisfied, depending on nothing, though engaged in action, he does not do anything."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Forsaking the clinging to fruits of action … though engaged in action, he does not do anything."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 4, verse: "22",
              sanskrit: #"यदृच्छालाभसन्तुष्टो द्वन्द्वातीतो विमत्सरः ।\#nसमः सिद्धावसिद्धौ च कृत्वापि न निबध्यते ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna paints a picture of inner freedom: content with whatever comes without strain, beyond the pull of opposites like pleasure and pain, free from envy, and the same in success and failure. Such a person acts but is not bound. The lesson is that contentment and evenness of mind let us live fully in the world without being trapped by it."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Content with what comes to him without effort, unaffected by the pairs of opposites, free from envy, even-minded in success and failure, though acting, he is not bound."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Content with what comes to him without effort … though acting, he is not bound."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 4, verse: "34",
              sanskrit: #"तद्विद्धि प्रणिपातेन परिप्रश्नेन सेवया ।\#nउपदेक्ष्यन्ति ते ज्ञानं ज्ञानिनस्तत्त्वदर्शिनः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna tells Arjuna how to gain true knowledge: approach those who have realised the truth, with humility, sincere questions and a spirit of service, and they will teach you. Wisdom is not just information to be collected. The lesson is that we learn most deeply when we come to a good teacher with an open heart, honest questions and a willingness to serve."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Know that, by prostrating thyself, by questions, and by service; the wise, those who have realised the Truth, will instruct thee in that knowledge."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"By prostrating thyself, by questions, and by service … the wise … will instruct thee."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 4, verse: "36",
              sanskrit: #"अपि चेदसि पापेभ्यः सर्वेभ्यः पापकृत्तमः ।\#nसर्वं ज्ञानप्लवेनैव वृजिनं सन्तरिष्यसि ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"This is one of the Gita's most hopeful verses. Krishna says that even if someone were the worst of all wrongdoers, the raft of knowledge would carry them safely across all their wrongs. No one is beyond redemption. The lesson is that our past does not have the last word: understanding and change are open to everyone."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Even if thou be the most sinful among all the sinful, yet by the raft of knowledge alone thou shalt go across all sin."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Even if thou be the most sinful … by the raft of knowledge alone thou shalt go across all sin."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 4, verse: "37",
              sanskrit: #"यथैधांसि समिद्धोऽग्निर्भस्मसात्कुरुतेऽर्जुन ।\#nज्ञानाग्निः सर्वकर्माणि भस्मसात्कुरुते तथा ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna gives a powerful image: just as a blazing fire turns wood to ash, the fire of knowledge burns up the bonds of past action. Karma that seemed to weigh us down loses its hold when we truly understand. The lesson is that wisdom is transforming, and that clear understanding can free us from the weight of what we have done."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"As blazing fire reduces wood into ashes, so, O Arjuna, does the fire of knowledge reduce all Karma to ashes."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"As blazing fire reduces wood … so … does the fire of knowledge reduce all Karma to ashes."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 4, verse: "38",
              sanskrit: #"न हि ज्ञानेन सदृशं पवित्रमिह विद्यते ।\#nतत्स्वयं योगसंसिद्धः कालेनात्मनि विन्दति ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Near the end of Chapter 4, Krishna praises knowledge, meaning true understanding of the Self rather than mere information. He has just said that the fire of knowledge burns all past actions to ashes. Now he declares that nothing in this world purifies like knowledge, and that one who is perfected in yoga finds it within, in time. The lesson is that inner understanding transforms a person more deeply than any outer ritual."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Verily there exists nothing in this world purifying like knowledge. In good time, having reached perfection in Yoga, one realises that oneself in one's own heart."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Verily there exists nothing in this world purifying like knowledge."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 4, verse: "39",
              sanskrit: #"श्रद्धावाँल्लभते ज्ञानं तत्परः संयतेन्द्रियः ।\#nज्ञानं लब्ध्वा परां शान्तिमचिरेणाधिगच्छति ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna is closing his teaching on knowledge in Chapter 4. He says it is gained by someone who has faith, is devoted to it, and has mastered their senses; having gained it, they quickly reach supreme peace. In the following verses he warns that the doubting mind finds happiness nowhere. The lesson is that sincerity, commitment and self-control open the door to understanding, and understanding leads to peace."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"The man with Shraddhâ, the devoted, the master of one's senses, attains (this) knowledge. Having attained knowledge one goes at once to the Supreme Peace."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Having attained knowledge one goes at once to the Supreme Peace."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 4, verse: "40",
              sanskrit: #"अज्ञश्चाश्रद्दधानश्च संशयात्मा विनश्यति ।\#nनायं लोकोऽस्ति न परो न सुखं संशयात्मनः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna warns about a corrosive kind of doubt: the person who neither knows nor trusts, and doubts everything, finds no happiness in this world or the next. This is not honest questioning, which the Gita welcomes, but a doubt that refuses to commit to anything. The lesson is that endless doubt paralyses us, and that some trust is needed before we can grow."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"The ignorant, the man without Shraddhâ, the doubting self, goes to destruction. The doubting self has neither this world, nor the next, nor happiness."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"The doubting self has neither this world, nor the next, nor happiness."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 4, verse: "42",
              sanskrit: #"तस्मादज्ञानसंभूतं हृत्स्थं ज्ञानासिनाऽऽत्मनः ।\#nछित्त्वैनं संशयं योगमातिष्ठोत्तिष्ठ भारत ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Chapter 4 ends with a call to action. Krishna tells Arjuna to cut through the doubt in his heart, born of ignorance, with the sword of knowledge, take his stand in yoga, and rise. After all the teaching, the point is to act. The lesson is that understanding should lead to courage: once we see clearly, we must stand up and do what is ours to do."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Therefore, cutting with the sword of knowledge, this doubt about the Self, born of ignorance, residing in thy heart, take refuge in Yoga. Arise, O Bhârata!"#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Cutting with the sword of knowledge, this doubt … take refuge in Yoga. Arise, O Bhârata!"#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 5, verse: "3",
              sanskrit: #"ज्ञेयः स नित्यसंन्यासी यो न द्वेष्टि न काङ्क्षति ।\#nनिर्द्वन्द्वो हि महाबाहो सुखं बन्धात्प्रमुच्यते ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Arjuna has asked whether renunciation or action is better. Krishna says the true renunciate is the one who neither hates nor craves, not necessarily the one who has left home. Free from these pairs of opposites, such a person is easily released from bondage. The lesson is that renunciation is an inner attitude, not an outer costume."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"He should be known a constant Sannyâsi, who neither likes nor dislikes: for, free from the pairs of opposites, mighty-armed, he is easily set free from bondage."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"He should be known a constant Sannyâsi, who neither likes nor dislikes."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 5, verse: "10",
              sanskrit: #"ब्रह्मण्याधाय कर्माणि सङ्गं त्यक्त्वा करोति यः ।\#nलिप्यते न स पापेन पद्मपत्रमिवाम्भसा ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna uses one of the Gita's best-loved images. A lotus leaf sits in water but stays dry, because water simply rolls off it. In the same way, someone who acts without attachment, offering their work to the divine, lives in the world without being stained by it. The lesson is that we can be fully involved in life and still remain inwardly untouched."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"He who does actions forsaking attachment, resigning them to Brahman, is not soiled by evil, like unto a lotus-leaf by water."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"He who does actions forsaking attachment … is not soiled by evil, like … a lotus-leaf by water."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 5, verse: "12",
              sanskrit: #"युक्तः कर्मफलं त्यक्त्वा शान्तिमाप्नोति नैष्ठिकीम् ।\#nअयुक्तः कामकारेण फले सक्तो निबध्यते ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna contrasts two people. The steady one gives up attachment to results and finds a peace born of that steadiness; the unsteady one, driven by desire, clings to results and is bound by them. The lesson is that peace does not come from getting the outcomes we want, but from loosening our grip on outcomes altogether."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"The well-poised, forsaking the fruit of action, attains peace, born of steadfastness; the unbalanced one, led by desire, is bound by being attached to the fruit (of action)."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"The well-poised, forsaking the fruit of action, attains peace, born of steadfastness."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 5, verse: "18",
              sanskrit: #"विद्याविनयसम्पन्ने ब्राह्मणे गवि हस्तिनि ।\#nशुनि चैव श्वपाके च पण्डिताः समदर्शिनः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"In Chapter 5, Arjuna asks whether renouncing action or performing it is better, and Krishna explains how a truly wise person sees the world. Such a person looks with an equal eye on a learned priest, a cow, an elephant, a dog, and an outcaste. In a society divided by caste and status, this was a radical statement. The lesson is that wisdom sees the same divine Self in every being, and respects them all."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"The knowers of the Self look with an equal eye on a Brâhmana endowed with learning and humility, a cow, an elephant, a dog, and a pariah."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"The knowers of the Self look with an equal eye on a Brâhmana … a dog, and a pariah."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 5, verse: "20",
              sanskrit: #"न प्रहृष्येत्प्रियं प्राप्य नोद्विजेत्प्राप्य चाप्रियम् ।\#nस्थिरबुद्धिरसम्मूढो ब्रह्मविद्ब्रह्मणि स्थितः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna describes someone established in the highest reality: their understanding is steady and clear, and they neither rejoice too much at what is pleasant nor grieve at what is unpleasant. The lesson is that a mind rooted in something deeper is not carried away by every pleasant or painful event."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Resting in Brahman, with intellect steady, and without delusion, the knower of Brahman neither rejoiceth on receiving what is pleasant, nor grieveth on receiving what is unpleasant."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"The knower of Brahman neither rejoiceth … nor grieveth on receiving what is unpleasant."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 5, verse: "21",
              sanskrit: #"बाह्यस्पर्शेष्वसक्तात्मा विन्दत्यात्मनि यत्सुखम् ।\#nस ब्रह्मयोगयुक्तात्मा सुखमक्षयमश्नुते ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna says that the person whose heart is not tied to outer pleasures finds the joy that lies within, and, united with the highest through meditation, enjoys a happiness that does not fade. The lesson is that the deepest joy is not out there to be acquired; it is already within us, waiting to be uncovered."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"With the heart unattached to external objects, he realises the joy that is in the Self. With the heart devoted to the meditation of Brahman, he attains un-decaying happiness."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"With the heart unattached to external objects, he realises the joy that is in the Self."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 5, verse: "22",
              sanskrit: #"ये हि संस्पर्शजा भोगा दुःखयोनय एव ते ।\#nआद्यन्तवन्तः कौन्तेय न तेषु रमते बुधः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna looks honestly at sense pleasures: they arise from contact, they have a beginning and an end, and in the long run they are sources of sorrow. So the wise do not seek their happiness in them. This is not puritanical; it is clear-eyed. The lesson is that pleasures that depend on passing contact cannot give lasting happiness, so it is wise not to build our lives on them."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Since enjoyments that are contact-born are parents of misery alone, and with beginning and end, O son of Kunti, a wise man does not seek pleasure in them."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Enjoyments that are contact-born are parents of misery alone."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 5, verse: "23",
              sanskrit: #"शक्नोतीहैव यः सोढुं प्राक्शरीरविमोक्षणात् ।\#nकामक्रोधोद्भवं वेगं स युक्तः स सुखी नरः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna says that the person who, here in this life, can withstand the surge of desire and anger is truly disciplined and truly happy. Note that he does not say they never feel these forces, only that they can hold steady against them. The lesson is that happiness is closely tied to self-mastery, and that learning to withstand our strongest impulses is a form of freedom."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"He who can withstand in this world, before the liberation from the body, the impulse arising from lust and anger, he is steadfast (in Yoga), he is a happy man."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"He who can withstand … the impulse arising from lust and anger … he is a happy man."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 5, verse: "24",
              sanskrit: #"योऽन्तःसुखोऽन्तरारामस्तथान्तर्ज्योतिरेव यः ।\#nस योगी ब्रह्मनिर्वाणं ब्रह्मभूतोऽधिगच्छति ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"In a beautiful verse, Krishna describes the person whose happiness is within, whose delight is within, and whose light is within. Such a person becomes one with the highest and attains complete freedom. The lesson is that the most reliable source of happiness, rest and guidance is not outside us but in our own depths."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Whose happiness is within, whose relaxation is within, whose light is within, that Yogi alone, becoming Brahman, gains absolute freedom."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Whose happiness is within … whose light is within, that Yogi … gains absolute freedom."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 5, verse: "25",
              sanskrit: #"लभन्ते ब्रह्मनिर्वाणमृषयः क्षीणकल्मषाः ।\#nछिन्नद्वैधा यतात्मानः सर्वभूतहिते रताः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna describes the sages who reach complete freedom: their faults have worn away, their doubts are resolved, their senses are mastered, and they are devoted to the good of all beings. Liberation here is not selfish escape. The lesson is that inner freedom and care for others go together; the free person naturally works for everyone's welfare."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"With imperfections exhausted, doubts dispelled, senses controlled, engaged in the good of all beings, the Rishis obtain absolute freedom."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Engaged in the good of all beings, the Rishis obtain absolute freedom."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 5, verse: "29",
              sanskrit: #"भोक्तारं यज्ञतपसां सर्वलोकमहेश्वरम् ।\#nसुहृदं सर्वभूतानां ज्ञात्वा मां शान्तिमृच्छति ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Chapter 5 ends with a tender idea. Krishna says that whoever knows him as the receiver of all offerings, the great Lord of all the worlds, and the friend of all beings, attains peace. The divine is presented not as distant or stern but as a friend. The lesson is that knowing ourselves held by a friendly presence at the heart of things brings deep peace."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Knowing Me as the dispenser of Yajnas and asceticisms, as the Great Lord of all worlds, as the friend of all beings, he attains Peace."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Knowing Me … as the friend of all beings, he attains Peace."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 6, verse: "1",
              sanskrit: #"अनाश्रितः कर्मफलं कार्यं कर्म करोति यः ।\#nस संन्यासी च योगी च न निरग्निर्न चाक्रियः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Chapter 6 opens by redefining renunciation. Krishna says the true renunciate and true yogi is the person who does the work that is theirs to do without depending on its results, not the one who simply gives up rituals or stops acting. The lesson is that spiritual life is not about withdrawing from responsibility but about doing our duty with a free heart."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"He who performs his bounden duty without leaning to the fruit of action—he is a renouncer of action as well as of steadfast mind: not he who is without fire, nor he who is without action."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"He who performs his bounden duty without leaning to the fruit … is a renouncer of action."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 6, verse: "5",
              sanskrit: #"उद्धरेदात्मनाऽऽत्मानं नात्मानमवसादयेत् ।\#nआत्मैव ह्यात्मनो बन्धुरात्मैव रिपुरात्मनः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Chapter 6 is Krishna's teaching on meditation and self-mastery. He begins with a striking idea: lift yourself up by your own effort, and don't drag yourself down. Your own mind can be your greatest friend or your worst enemy. The lesson is self-responsibility: no one else can do the inner work for us, but we have the power to raise ourselves."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"A man should uplift himself by his own self, so let him not weaken this self. For this self is the friend of oneself, and this self is the enemy of oneself."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"A man should uplift himself by his own self, so let him not weaken this self."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 6, verse: "6",
              sanskrit: #"बन्धुरात्माऽऽत्मनस्तस्य येनात्मैवात्मना जितः ।\#nअनात्मनस्तु शत्रुत्वे वर्तेतात्मैव शत्रुवत् ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna explains the previous verse. For someone who has conquered their mind, the mind becomes their best friend; for someone who hasn't, it behaves like an enemy. Arjuna's present crisis is a vivid example of a mind turned against itself. The lesson is that training the mind is a kind of friendship we build with ourselves over time."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"The self (the active part of our nature) is the friend of the self, for him who has conquered himself by this self. But to the unconquered self, this self is inimical, (and behaves) like (an external) foe."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"The self … is the friend of the self, for him who has conquered himself by this self."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 6, verse: "9",
              sanskrit: #"सुहृन्मित्रार्युदासीनमध्यस्थद्वेष्यबन्धुषु ।\#nसाधुष्वपि च पापेषु समबुद्धिर्विशिष्यते ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna says that the person who regards everyone with an equal eye, well-wishers, friends, enemies, neutrals, the hateful, relatives, the good and the wicked, has reached excellence. This does not mean liking everyone the same, but being fair and free of prejudice towards all. The lesson is that true maturity shows in how evenly we treat people, especially those we find difficult."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"He attains excellence who looks with equal regard upon well-wishers, friends, foes, neutrals, arbiters, the hateful, the relatives, and upon the righteous and the unrighteous alike."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"He attains excellence who looks with equal regard upon … friends, foes … alike."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 6, verse: "16",
              sanskrit: #"नात्यश्नतस्तु योगोऽस्ति न चैकान्तमनश्नतः ।\#nन चातिस्वप्नशीलस्य जाग्रतो नैव चार्जुन ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"In the middle of his teaching on meditation, Krishna gives very practical advice: yoga is not for those who eat too much or too little, or sleep too much or too little. Extremes in either direction undermine the inner life. The lesson is moderation: a balanced, sensible daily life is the foundation for any deeper practice."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"(Success in) Yoga is not for him who eats too much or too little—nor, O Arjuna, for him who sleeps too much or too little."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Yoga is not for him who eats too much or too little … who sleeps too much or too little."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 6, verse: "17",
              sanskrit: #"युक्ताहारविहारस्य युक्तचेष्टस्य कर्मसु ।\#nयुक्तस्वप्नावबोधस्य योगो भवति दुःखहा ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"In his practical instructions on meditation, Krishna warns against extremes. Yoga, he says just before this, is not for those who eat too much or too little, or who sleep too much or too little. Here he says yoga ends sorrow for someone who is moderate in eating, recreation, work, and sleep and waking. The lesson is balance: spiritual growth comes from a steady, sensible life, not from harsh deprivation or excess."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"To him who is temperate in eating and recreation, in his effort for work, and in sleep and wakefulness, Yoga becomes the destroyer of misery."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"To him who is temperate … Yoga becomes the destroyer of misery."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 6, verse: "19",
              sanskrit: #"यथा दीपो निवातस्थो नेङ्गते सोपमा स्मृता ।\#nयोगिनो यतचित्तस्य युञ्जतो योगमात्मनः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna is describing the mind of a person deep in meditation. He gives a beautiful image: like a lamp in a windless place whose flame does not flicker. The winds are the constant pull of the senses and desires. The lesson is that a calm, sheltered mind can burn steadily and brightly, and creating that shelter is the purpose of practice."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"“As a lamp in a spot sheltered from the wind does not flicker,”—even such has been the simile used for a Yogi of subdued mind, practising concentration in the Self."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"As a lamp … sheltered from the wind does not flicker,—even such … a Yogi of subdued mind."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 6, verse: "26",
              sanskrit: #"यतो यतो निश्चरति मनश्चञ्चलमस्थिरम् ।\#nततस्ततो नियम्यैतदात्मन्येव वशं नयेत् ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna is giving very practical advice on meditation. Whenever the restless, wandering mind slips away, gently bring it back and settle it in the Self, again and again. This is the same instruction meditation teachers give today. The lesson is that wandering is normal; what matters is patiently returning."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Through whatever reason the restless, unsteady mind wanders away, let him curbing it from that, bring it under the subjugation of the Self alone."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"The restless, unsteady mind wanders away … bring it under the subjugation of the Self alone."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 6, verse: "27",
              sanskrit: #"प्रशान्तमनसं ह्येनं योगिनं सुखमुत्तमम् ।\#nउपैति शान्तरजसं ब्रह्मभूतमकल्मषम् ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna describes the fruit of meditation: supreme happiness comes to the yogi whose mind is completely calm, whose passions have settled, who is free from wrongdoing and has become one with the highest. The lesson is that the deepest joy is found in stillness, when restless desires quiet down and the mind rests in its source."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Verily, the supreme bliss comes to that Yogi, of perfectly tranquil mind, with passions quieted, Brahman-become, and freed from taint."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"The supreme bliss comes to that Yogi, of perfectly tranquil mind, with passions quieted."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 6, verse: "29",
              sanskrit: #"सर्वभूतस्थमात्मानं सर्वभूतानि चात्मनि ।\#nईक्षते योगयुक्तात्मा सर्वत्र समदर्शनः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna describes the vision of the one whose mind is united in yoga: they see the Self in all beings and all beings in the Self, and look on everything with an equal eye. Differences remain, but the deeper unity is clear. The lesson is that seeing ourselves in others, and others in ourselves, is a sign of true spiritual sight."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"With the heart concentrated by Yoga, with the eye of evenness for all things, he beholds the Self in all beings and all beings in the Self."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"He beholds the Self in all beings and all beings in the Self."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 6, verse: "30",
              sanskrit: #"यो मां पश्यति सर्वत्र सर्वं च मयि पश्यति ।\#nतस्याहं न प्रणश्यामि स च मे न प्रणश्यति ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Building on the previous verse, Krishna promises that whoever sees him everywhere, and sees everything in him, is never lost to him, and he is never lost to them. It is a verse of deep intimacy. The lesson is that when we learn to see the divine in all things, we are never truly separated from it, wherever we are."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"He who sees Me in all things, and sees all things in Me, he never becomes separated from Me, nor do I become separated from him."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"He who sees Me in all things, and sees all things in Me, he never becomes separated from Me."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 6, verse: "32",
              sanskrit: #"आत्मौपम्येन सर्वत्र समं पश्यति योऽर्जुन ।\#nसुखं वा यदि वा दुःखं सः योगी परमो मतः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna names the highest yogi: the one who judges the pleasure and pain of others by the same standard they apply to themselves. Others' joy and suffering feel as real as their own. The lesson is that the summit of spiritual practice is not some private experience, but deep empathy, treating others' wellbeing as we treat our own."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"He who judges of pleasure or pain everywhere, by the same standard as he applies to himself, that Yogi, O Arjuna, is regarded as the highest."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"He who judges … by the same standard as he applies to himself … is regarded as the highest."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 6, verse: "34",
              sanskrit: #"चञ्चलं हि मनः कृष्ण प्रमाथि बलवद्दृढम् ।\#nतस्याहं निग्रहं मन्ये वायोरिव सुदुष्करम् ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Here Arjuna speaks with refreshing honesty. The mind, he tells Krishna, is restless, turbulent, strong and stubborn, and controlling it seems as hard as controlling the wind. Anyone who has tried to meditate will recognise this. Krishna's reply, in the next verse, is that it can be done through practice and detachment. The lesson is that struggling with the mind is normal, and admitting it is the first step."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Verily, the mind, O Krishna, is restless, turbulent, strong, and unyielding; I regard it quite as hard to achieve its control, as that of the wind."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"The mind … is restless … I regard it quite as hard to achieve its control, as that of the wind."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 6, verse: "35",
              sanskrit: #"असंशयं महाबाहो मनो दुर्निग्रहं चलम् ।\#nअभ्यासेन तु कौन्तेय वैराग्येण च गृह्यते ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"After hearing about meditation, Arjuna objects honestly: the mind is restless, stubborn and as hard to control as the wind. Krishna doesn't deny it; he agrees that the mind is difficult to restrain. But he says it can be trained through practice (abhyasa) and detachment (vairagya). The lesson is that our struggles with the mind are real and shared, and steady practice is the answer."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Without doubt, O mighty-armed, the mind is restless, and difficult to control; but through practice and renunciation, O son of Kunti, it may be governed."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"The mind is restless … but through practice and renunciation … it may be governed."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 6, verse: "36",
              sanskrit: #"असंयतात्मना योगो दुष्प्राप इति मे मतिः ।\#nवश्यात्मना तु यतता शक्योऽवाप्तुमुपायतः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna agrees with Arjuna that yoga is hard for someone with no self-control. But, he says, for the self-disciplined person who keeps striving by the right means, it can be achieved. Difficulty is not impossibility. The lesson is that the inner life is demanding, yet steady, well-directed effort really does bear fruit."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Yoga is hard to be attained by one of uncontrolled self: such is My conviction; but the self-controlled, striving by right means, can obtain it."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Yoga is hard to be attained by one of uncontrolled self … the self-controlled … can obtain it."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 6, verse: "40",
              sanskrit: #"पार्थ नैवेह नामुत्र विनाशस्तस्य विद्यते ।\#nन हि कल्याणकृत्कश्चिद्दुर्गतिं तात गच्छति ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Arjuna asks a worried question: what happens to someone who has faith but fails in their practice? Are they lost, like a cloud torn apart in the sky? Krishna answers warmly that such a person is never destroyed, in this world or the next. No one who does good ever comes to a bad end. The lesson is deep reassurance: sincere effort is never wasted, even when we fall short."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Verily, O son of Prithâ, there is destruction for him, neither here nor hereafter: for, the doer of good, O my son, never comes to grief."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"The doer of good, O my son, never comes to grief."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 6, verse: "45",
              sanskrit: #"प्रयत्नाद्यतमानस्तु योगी संशुद्धकिल्बिषः ।\#nअनेकजन्मसंसिद्धस्ततो याति परां गतिम् ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Arjuna has worried about what happens to a seeker who fails. Krishna reassures him: the yogi who strives earnestly, gradually purified and perfected over many lifetimes, reaches the highest goal in the end. No sincere effort is wasted. The lesson is patience: spiritual growth can be slow, but every honest step counts and carries us forward."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"The Yogi, striving assiduously, purified of taint, gradually gaining perfection through many births, then reaches the highest goal."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"The Yogi, striving assiduously … through many births, then reaches the highest goal."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 7, verse: "3",
              sanskrit: #"मनुष्याणां सहस्रेषु कश्चिद्यतति सिद्धये ।\#nयततामपि सिद्धानां कश्चिन्मां वेत्ति तत्त्वतः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna is honest about how rare real spiritual attainment is. Among thousands of people, perhaps one strives for perfection, and among those who strive, perhaps one truly knows him. This is not meant to discourage, but to put the path in perspective. The lesson is that the inner journey is demanding and uncommon, so if it feels hard, that is normal, and simply striving already sets us apart."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"One, perchance, in thousands of men, strives for perfection; and one perchance, among the blessed ones, striving thus, knows Me in reality."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"One, perchance, in thousands of men, strives for perfection."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 7, verse: "7",
              sanskrit: #"मत्तः परतरं नान्यत्किञ्चिदस्ति धनञ्जय ।\#nमयि सर्वमिदं प्रोतं सूत्रे मणिगणा इव ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna says there is nothing higher than him, and that everything is strung on him like jewels on a thread. The jewels look separate, but one hidden thread holds them all together. The lesson is that beneath the variety of the world there is a single underlying reality connecting everything, even when we cannot see it."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Beyond Me, O Dhananjaya, there is naught. All this is strung in Me, as a row of jewels on a thread."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"All this is strung in Me, as a row of jewels on a thread."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 7, verse: "8",
              sanskrit: #"रसोऽहमप्सु कौन्तेय प्रभास्मि शशिसूर्ययोः ।\#nप्रणवः सर्ववेदेषु शब्दः खे पौरुषं नृषु ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna begins to show how he can be found in ordinary things: he is the taste in water, the light of the moon and the sun, the sacred syllable Om, the sound in space, and the strength in human beings. The divine is not only in temples or scriptures. The lesson is that the sacred is present in the simplest things around us, if we learn to notice it."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"I am the sapidity in waters, O son of Kunti; I, the radiance in the moon and the sun; I am the Om in all the Vedas, sound in Akâsha, and manhood in men."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"I am the sapidity in waters … the radiance in the moon and the sun."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 7, verse: "17",
              sanskrit: #"तेषां ज्ञानी नित्ययुक्त एकभक्तिर्विशिष्यते ।\#nप्रियो हि ज्ञानिनोऽत्यर्थमहं स च मम प्रियः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna has described four kinds of people who turn to him: the distressed, the seeker of knowledge, the seeker of gain, and the wise. Of these, he says, the wise one, steady and devoted to the One, is best, and the love between them is mutual: he is dear to the wise and the wise are dear to him. The lesson is that understanding combined with wholehearted love is the highest form of devotion."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Of them, the wise man, ever-steadfast, (and fired) with devotion to the One, excels; for supremely dear am I to the wise, and he is dear to Me."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"The wise man … excels; for supremely dear am I to the wise, and he is dear to Me."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 7, verse: "19",
              sanskrit: #"बहूनां जन्मनामन्ते ज्ञानवान्मां प्रपद्यते ।\#nवासुदेवः सर्वमिति स महात्मा सुदुर्लभः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna says that only at the end of many lifetimes does the wise person take refuge in him, realising that the divine is everything. Such a great soul, he adds, is very rare. The lesson is that this kind of all-embracing realisation is the fruit of a long journey, and that patience and perseverance are part of the path."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"At the end of many births, the man of wisdom takes refuge in Me, realising that all this is Vâsudeva (the innermost Self). Very rare is that great soul."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"At the end of many births, the man of wisdom takes refuge in Me … Very rare is that great soul."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 7, verse: "21",
              sanskrit: #"यो यो यां यां तनुं भक्तः श्रद्धयार्चितुमिच्छति ।\#nतस्य तस्याचलां श्रद्धां तामेव विदधाम्यहम् ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna makes a remarkable statement: whatever form a devotee chooses to worship with sincere faith, he himself makes that faith steady. He does not demand that everyone worship in one way. The lesson is respect for the many paths people take, and trust that sincere faith, in whatever form, is supported by the divine."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Whatsoever form any devotee seeks to worship with Shraddhâ,—that Shraddhâ of his do I make unwavering."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Whatsoever form any devotee seeks to worship … that Shraddhâ of his do I make unwavering."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 8, verse: "6",
              sanskrit: #"यं यं वापि स्मरन्भावं त्यजत्यन्ते कलेवरम् ।\#nतं तमेवैति कौन्तेय सदा तद्भावभावितः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna says that whatever a person is thinking of at the moment of death, that is what they go to, because they have been constantly absorbed in it. The last thought is not random; it reflects what has filled the mind all along. The lesson is that what we dwell on day after day shapes who we become, so it matters where we habitually place our attention."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Remembering whatever object, at the end, he leaves the body, that alone is reached by him, O son of Kunti, (because) of his constant thought of that object."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Remembering whatever object, at the end, he leaves the body, that alone is reached by him."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 8, verse: "7",
              sanskrit: #"तस्मात्सर्वेषु कालेषु मामनुस्मर युध्य च ।\#nमय्यर्पितमनोबुद्धिर्मामेवैष्यस्यसंशयम् ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Following from the previous verse, Krishna tells Arjuna to remember him at all times, and fight. He does not ask Arjuna to leave his duty to become holy; he asks him to do both together. The lesson is that spiritual life is not separate from everyday responsibilities: we can keep the highest in mind while doing the work in front of us."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Therefore, at all times, constantly remember Me, and fight. With mind and intellect absorbed, in Me, thou shalt doubtless come to Me."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"At all times, constantly remember Me, and fight."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 8, verse: "14",
              sanskrit: #"अनन्यचेताः सततं यो मां स्मरति नित्यशः ।\#nतस्याहं सुलभः पार्थ नित्ययुक्तस्य योगिनः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna says he is easily reached by the person who remembers him constantly, every day, with an undivided mind. After much talk of difficult practices, this is a simple and encouraging message. The lesson is that steady, loving remembrance, practised daily, is a direct and accessible path to the divine."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"I am easily attainable by that ever-steadfast Yogin who remembers Me constantly and daily, with a single mind, O son of Prithâ."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"I am easily attainable by that ever-steadfast Yogin who remembers Me constantly and daily."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 9, verse: "4",
              sanskrit: #"मया ततमिदं सर्वं जगदव्यक्तमूर्तिना ।\#nमत्स्थानि सर्वभूतानि न चाहं तेष्ववस्थितः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna states a paradox: the whole universe is pervaded by him in his unseen form, all beings rest in him, yet he does not rest in them. He sustains everything without being limited or contained by any of it. The lesson is that the divine is both intimately present everywhere and infinitely greater than anything it supports."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"All this world is pervaded by me in My unmanifested form: all beings exist in Me, but I do not dwell in them."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"All this world is pervaded by me … all beings exist in Me, but I do not dwell in them."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 9, verse: "18",
              sanskrit: #"गतिर्भर्ता प्रभुः साक्षी निवासः शरणं सुहृत् ।\#nप्रभवः प्रलयः स्थानं निधानं बीजमव्ययम् ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"In a single sweep, Krishna names himself as the goal, the support, the lord, the witness, the home, the refuge, the friend, the origin and the end, the foundation, the storehouse and the imperishable seed. Each word is a different way of relating to him. The lesson is that whatever we are looking for, whether shelter, friendship, meaning or a final aim, can be found in the divine."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"The Goal, the Supporter, the Lord, the Witness, the Abode, the Refuge, the Friend, the Origin, the Dissolution, the Substratum, the Storehouse, the Seed immutable."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"The Goal, the Supporter … the Refuge, the Friend … the Seed immutable."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 9, verse: "22",
              sanskrit: #"अनन्याश्चिन्तयन्तो मां ये जनाः पर्युपासते ।\#nतेषां नित्याभियुक्तानां योगक्षेमं वहाम्यहम् ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"In Chapter 9, Krishna shares what he calls the most secret knowledge, the path of loving devotion. He has just contrasted people who perform rituals to gain heaven, whose rewards run out, with those who worship him alone. To those who think of him constantly and are always devoted, he promises to bring what they lack and protect what they have. The lesson is trust: a life centred on the divine is supported and cared for."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Persons who, meditating on Me as non-separate, worship Me in all beings, to them thus ever jealously engaged, I carry what they lack and preserve what they already have."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Persons who … worship Me … I carry what they lack and preserve what they already have."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 9, verse: "26",
              sanskrit: #"पत्रं पुष्पं फलं तोयं यो मे भक्त्या प्रयच्छति ।\#nतदहं भक्त्युपहृतमश्नामि प्रयतात्मनः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Continuing his teaching on devotion, Krishna says something beautifully simple. Whoever offers him with love a leaf, a flower, a fruit or a little water, he accepts it. Elaborate rituals and wealth are not required. The lesson is that what matters is the love behind an offering, not its size or cost."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Whoever with devotion offers Me a leaf, a flower, a fruit, or water, that I accept—the devout gift of the pure-minded."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Whoever with devotion offers Me a leaf, a flower, a fruit, or water, that I accept."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 9, verse: "27",
              sanskrit: #"यत्करोषि यदश्नासि यज्जुहोषि ददासि यत् ।\#nयत्तपस्यसि कौन्तेय तत्कुरुष्व मदर्पणम् ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Right after saying that a simple offering made with love is accepted, Krishna widens the idea to all of life. Whatever you do, eat, offer, give, or practise, do it as an offering to him. This turns everyday activity into worship. The lesson is that any ordinary moment can become sacred when it is done with dedication."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Whatever thou doest, whatever thou eatest, whatever thou offerest in sacrifice, whatever thou givest away, whatever austerity thou practisest, O son of Kunti, do that as an offering unto Me."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Whatever thou doest, whatever thou eatest … do that as an offering unto Me."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 9, verse: "29",
              sanskrit: #"समोऽहं सर्वभूतेषु न मे द्वेष्योऽस्ति न प्रियः ।\#nये भजन्ति तु मां भक्त्या मयि ते तेषु चाप्यहम् ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna says he is the same towards all beings: no one is hateful to him and no one is his favourite. Yet those who worship him with love are in him, and he is in them. Closeness is not favouritism on his side, but a response to love on ours. The lesson is that the divine is impartial, and that devotion is what draws us near."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"I am the same to all beings: to Me there is none hateful or dear. But those who worship Me with devotion, are in Me, and I too am in them."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"I am the same to all beings: to Me there is none hateful or dear."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 9, verse: "30",
              sanskrit: #"अपि चेत्सुदुराचारो भजते मामनन्यभाक् ।\#nसाधुरेव स मन्तव्यः सम्यग्व्यवसितो हि सः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"This is one of the Gita's most forgiving verses. Krishna says that even if a person of very bad conduct turns to him with undivided devotion, they should be regarded as good, because they have made the right resolve. The lesson is that a sincere change of heart matters more than our past, and that no one is shut out from the possibility of goodness."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"If even a very wicked person worships Me, with devotion to none else, he should be regarded as good, for he has rightly resolved."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"If even a very wicked person worships Me … he should be regarded as good."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 9, verse: "31",
              sanskrit: #"क्षिप्रं भवति धर्मात्मा शश्वच्छान्तिं निगच्छति ।\#nकौन्तेय प्रतिजानीहि न मे भक्तः प्रणश्यति ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Continuing from the previous verse, Krishna says that such a person soon becomes righteous and reaches lasting peace, and he tells Arjuna to proclaim it boldly: his devotee never perishes. It is a confident promise. The lesson is that sincere devotion transforms a person quickly, and that those who turn to the divine are never truly lost."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Soon does he become righteous, and attain eternal Peace, O son of Kunti; boldly canst thou proclaim, that My devotee is never destroyed."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Boldly canst thou proclaim, that My devotee is never destroyed."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 9, verse: "34",
              sanskrit: #"मन्मना भव मद्भक्तो मद्याजी मां नमस्कुरु ।\#nमामेवैष्यसि युक्त्वैवमात्मानं मत्परायणः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Chapter 9 ends with a direct, intimate instruction: fill your mind with me, be devoted to me, worship me, bow to me, and making me your goal, you will come to me. The same words return almost exactly near the end of the Gita. The lesson is that the whole spiritual path can be summed up as turning the heart, mind and actions wholly towards the divine."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Fill thy mind with Me, be My devotee, sacrifice unto Me, bow down to Me; thus having made thy heart steadfast in Me, taking Me as the Supreme Goal, thou shalt come to Me."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Fill thy mind with Me, be My devotee … bow down to Me … thou shalt come to Me."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 10, verse: "8",
              sanskrit: #"अहं सर्वस्य प्रभवो मत्तः सर्वं प्रवर्तते ।\#nइति मत्वा भजन्ते मां बुधा भावसमन्विताः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna declares that he is the source of everything and that all things come from him. Those who understand this, he says, worship him with love and deep feeling. Knowing is meant to lead to loving. The lesson is that realising where everything comes from naturally fills the heart with gratitude and devotion."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"I am the origin of all, from Me everything evolves;—thus thinking the wise worship Me with loving consciousness."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"I am the origin of all, from Me everything evolves."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 10, verse: "11",
              sanskrit: #"तेषामेवानुकम्पार्थमहमज्ञानजं तमः ।\#nनाशयाम्यात्मभावस्थो ज्ञानदीपेन भास्वता ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna describes his care for his devotees. Out of pure compassion, dwelling in their hearts, he destroys the darkness born of ignorance with the shining lamp of knowledge. The light does not come from far away; it is lit from within. The lesson is that understanding is a gift of grace as much as effort, and that the divine works quietly within us to dispel confusion."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Out of mere compassion for them, I, abiding in their hearts, destroy the darkness (in them) born of ignorance, by the luminous lamp of knowledge."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Out of mere compassion for them, I … destroy the darkness … by the luminous lamp of knowledge."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 10, verse: "20",
              sanskrit: #"अहमात्मा गुडाकेश सर्वभूताशयस्थितः ।\#nअहमादिश्च मध्यं च भूतानामन्त एव च ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"In Chapter 10, Arjuna asks Krishna how he should think of him, and in what forms. Krishna begins listing his glories with this verse: he is the Self seated in the hearts of all beings, and their beginning, middle and end. He goes on to name the greatest of each kind of thing (the sun among lights, the ocean among waters) as reflections of himself. The lesson is that the divine is not far away; it lives at the heart of every being."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"I am the Self, O Gudâkesha, existent in the heart of all beings; I am the beginning, the middle, and also the end of all beings."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"I am the Self … existent in the heart of all beings."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 10, verse: "39",
              sanskrit: #"यच्चापि सर्वभूतानां बीजं तदहमर्जुन ।\#nन तदस्ति विना यत्स्यान्मया भूतं चराचरम् ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Near the end of describing his glories, Krishna says that he is the seed of all beings, and that nothing, moving or unmoving, could exist without him. Everything that lives draws its existence from him. The lesson is that every being, however small, depends on and expresses the same underlying source of life."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"And whatsoever is the seed of all beings, that also am I, O Arjuna. There is no being, whether moving or unmoving, that can exist without Me."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"There is no being, whether moving or unmoving, that can exist without Me."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 10, verse: "41",
              sanskrit: #"यद्यद्विभूतिमत्सत्त्वं श्रीमदूर्जितमेव वा ।\#nतत्तदेवावगच्छ त्वं मम तेजोंऽशसंभवम् ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna has been listing his manifestations in the world: the greatest mountain, river, sage and so on. Here he sums up: wherever you find anything glorious, flourishing or powerful, know that it springs from just a spark of his splendour. The lesson is to see excellence and beauty in the world as glimpses of the divine, and to honour them that way."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Whatever being there is great, prosperous or powerful, that know thou to be a product of a part of My splendour."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Whatever being there is great, prosperous or powerful … a product of a part of My splendour."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 10, verse: "42",
              sanskrit: #"अथवा बहुनैतेन किं ज्ञातेन तवार्जुन ।\#nविष्टभ्याहमिदं कृत्स्नमेकांशेन स्थितो जगत् ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Chapter 10 ends with a striking shift of scale. After all the examples, Krishna asks what use it is to know all these details, since he supports the entire universe with just a single fragment of himself. The lesson is that however vast the universe seems, the divine is greater still, and the details matter less than knowing this."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Or what avails thee to know all this diversity, O Arjuna? (Know thou this, that) I exist, supporting this whole world by a portion of Myself."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"I exist, supporting this whole world by a portion of Myself."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 11, verse: "12",
              sanskrit: #"दिवि सूर्यसहस्रस्य भवेद्युगपदुत्थिता ।\#nयदि भाः सदृशी सा स्याद्भासस्तस्य महात्मनः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Arjuna has been given divine sight and is seeing Krishna's universal form. The narrator Sanjaya tries to describe its brilliance: if the light of a thousand suns rose in the sky at once, it might resemble the splendour of that great being. The lesson is that the ultimate reality is so vast and dazzling that even our strongest images can only hint at it."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"If the splendour of a thousand suns were to rise up at once in the sky, that would be like the splendour of that Mighty Being."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"If the splendour of a thousand suns were to rise … that would be like … that Mighty Being."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 11, verse: "32",
              sanskrit: #"कालोऽस्मि लोकक्षयकृत्प्रवृद्धो लोकान्समाहर्तुमिह प्रवृत्तः ।\#nऋतेऽपि त्वां न भविष्यन्ति सर्वे येऽवस्थिताः प्रत्यनीकेषु योधाः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Arjuna, overwhelmed by the universal form, asks who Krishna really is. Krishna answers that he is Time, the mighty destroyer of worlds, and that even without Arjuna, the warriors on both sides will not survive. This is the verse physicist J. Robert Oppenheimer famously recalled after the first atomic test. The lesson is that time ends all things, and that we are not the ultimate controllers of events, only participants."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"I am the mighty world-destroying Time, here made manifest for the purpose of infolding the world. Even without thee, none of the warriors arrayed in the hostile armies shall live."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"I am the mighty world-destroying Time."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 11, verse: "44",
              sanskrit: #"तस्मात्प्रणम्य प्रणिधाय कायं प्रसादये त्वामहमीशमीड्यम् ।\#nपितेव पुत्रस्य सखेव सख्युः प्रियः प्रियायार्हसि देव सोढुम् ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Terrified and awed by the universal form, Arjuna bows down and asks Krishna's forgiveness for having treated him casually as a friend. He asks to be forgiven as a father forgives a son, a friend a friend, and a lover the beloved. The lesson is that even in the face of the infinite, the relationship can be close and tender, and forgiveness is part of that closeness."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"So prostrating my body in adoration, I crave Thy forgiveness, Lord adorable! As a father forgiveth his son, friend a dear friend, a beloved one his love, even so shouldst Thou forgive me, O Deva."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"As a father forgiveth his son, friend a dear friend … even so shouldst Thou forgive me."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 11, verse: "55",
              sanskrit: #"मत्कर्मकृन्मत्परमो मद्भक्तः सङ्गवर्जितः ।\#nनिर्वैरः सर्वभूतेषु यः स मामेति पाण्डव ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Chapter 11 ends with a verse often described as a summary of the whole Gita. Krishna says that whoever works for him, makes him their goal, is devoted to him, is free from attachment, and bears no hatred towards any being, comes to him. The lesson is that devotion, selfless action and goodwill towards all are, together, the heart of the spiritual path."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"He who does work for Me alone and has Me for his goal, is devoted to Me, is freed from attachment, and bears enmity towards no creature—he entereth into Me, O Pândava."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"He who does work for Me alone … and bears enmity towards no creature—he entereth into Me."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 12, verse: "8",
              sanskrit: #"मय्येव मन आधत्स्व मयि बुद्धिं निवेशय ।\#nनिवसिष्यसि मय्येव अत ऊर्ध्वं न संशयः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna begins a series of options for different kinds of seekers, starting with the highest: fix your mind on me alone, rest your understanding in me, and you will live in me from then on. If this is too hard, he goes on to offer easier steps. The lesson is that the ideal is complete absorption in the divine, but the path meets us wherever we are."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Fix thy mind on Me only, place thy intellect in Me: (then) thou shalt no doubt live in Me hereafter."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Fix thy mind on Me only, place thy intellect in Me."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 12, verse: "12",
              sanskrit: #"श्रेयो हि ज्ञानमभ्यासाज्ज्ञानाद्ध्यानं विशिष्यते ।\#nध्यानात्कर्मफलत्यागस्त्यागाच्छान्तिरनन्तरम् ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna ranks the steps he has been offering: understanding is better than mechanical practice, meditation is better than mere understanding, and letting go of the results of action is better than meditation, because peace follows immediately from letting go. The lesson is that releasing our grip on outcomes is one of the quickest ways to peace, available to anyone."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Better indeed is knowledge than (blind) Abhyâsa; meditation (with knowledge) is more esteemed than (mere) knowledge; than meditation the renunciation of the fruit of action; peace immediately follows renunciation."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Peace immediately follows renunciation."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 12, verse: "13–14",
              sanskrit: #"अद्वेष्टा सर्वभूतानां मैत्रः करुण एव च ।\#nनिर्ममो निरहङ्कारः समदुःखसुखः क्षमी ॥\#nसन्तुष्टः सततं योगी यतात्मा दृढनिश्चयः ।\#nमय्यर्पितमनोबुद्धिर्यो मद्भक्तः स मे प्रियः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"In Chapter 12, Arjuna asks which devotees are better: those who worship God with form or without form. After answering, Krishna describes the qualities of the devotee most dear to him. These verses begin that list: someone who bears ill will to no one, who is friendly and compassionate, free from possessiveness and ego, balanced in pleasure and pain, forgiving, content and self-controlled. The lesson is that devotion is measured by character and kindness, not only by prayer."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"He who hates no creature, and is friendly and compassionate towards all, who is free from the feelings of 'I and mine,' even-minded in pain and pleasure, forbearing, ever content, steady in meditation, self-controlled, and possessed of firm conviction, with mind and intellect fixed on Me,—he who is thus devoted to Me, is dear to Me."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"He who hates no creature, and is friendly and compassionate towards all … is dear to Me."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 12, verse: "15",
              sanskrit: #"यस्मान्नोद्विजते लोको लोकान्नोद्विजते च यः ।\#nहर्षामर्षभयोद्वेगैर्मुक्तो यः स च मे प्रियः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna is continuing his description of the devotee who is dear to him. This person does not disturb the world, and is not disturbed by it; they are free from excitement, anger, fear and anxiety. It describes a quiet, steady presence that neither causes turmoil nor is thrown by it. The lesson is to be a source of peace for others while staying calm within."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"He by whom the world is not agitated and who cannot be agitated by the world, who is freed from joy, envy, fear and anxiety,—he is dear to Me."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"He by whom the world is not agitated … he is dear to Me."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 12, verse: "17",
              sanskrit: #"यो न हृष्यति न द्वेष्टि न शोचति न काङ्क्षति ।\#nशुभाशुभपरित्यागी भक्तिमान्यः स मे प्रियः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"In his description of the devotee who is dear to him, Krishna says such a person neither gets carried away with joy nor hates, neither grieves nor craves, has let go of both good and bad outcomes, and is full of devotion. The lesson is that loving devotion goes hand in hand with a balanced heart, free from the swings of excitement and resentment."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"He who neither rejoices, nor hates, nor grieves, nor desires, renouncing good and evil, full of devotion, he is dear to Me."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"He who neither rejoices, nor hates, nor grieves, nor desires … he is dear to Me."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 12, verse: "18–19",
              sanskrit: #"समः शत्रौ च मित्रे च तथा मानापमानयोः ।\#nशीतोष्णसुखदुःखेषु समः सङ्गविवर्जितः ॥\#nतुल्यनिन्दास्तुतिर्मौनी सन्तुष्टो येनकेनचित् ।\#nअनिकेतः स्थिरमतिर्भक्तिमान्मे प्रियो नरः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna continues his portrait of the devotee he loves: the same to friend and enemy, in honour and insult, in heat and cold, pleasure and pain; free from attachment; unmoved by praise or blame; quiet, content with whatever comes, not bound to any one place, and steady of mind. The lesson is that true devotion shows itself in a calm and even heart, whatever life brings."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"He who is the same to friend and foe, and also in honour and dishonour; who is the same in heat and cold, and in pleasure and pain; who is free from attachment; to whom censure and praise are equal; who is silent, content with anything, homeless, steady-minded, full of devotion,—that man is dear to Me."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"He who is the same to friend and foe … content with anything … that man is dear to Me."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 13, verse: "17",
              sanskrit: #"ज्योतिषामपि तज्ज्योतिस्तमसः परमुच्यते ।\#nज्ञानं ज्ञेयं ज्ञानगम्यं हृदि सर्वस्य विष्ठितम् ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna describes the ultimate reality as the light of all lights, beyond all darkness. It is knowledge itself, what is to be known, and the goal of all knowing, and it dwells in the heart of every being. The lesson is that the light we seek is not far away: it is the very source of our own awareness, present in everyone."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"The Light even of lights, It is said to be beyond darkness; Knowledge, and the One Thing to be known, the Goal of knowledge, dwelling in the hearts of all."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"The Light even of lights, It is said to be beyond darkness … dwelling in the hearts of all."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 13, verse: "27",
              sanskrit: #"समं सर्वेषु भूतेषु तिष्ठन्तं परमेश्वरम् ।\#nविनश्यत्स्वविनश्यन्तं यः पश्यति स पश्यति ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Chapter 13 explains the 'field' (the body and the material world) and the 'knower of the field' (the conscious Self). Krishna says that whoever sees the Supreme Lord present equally in all beings, the imperishable within the perishing, truly sees. In the next verse he adds that such a person does not harm themselves or others. The lesson is that real vision sees the one divine life in everyone, and that seeing it changes how we treat others."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"He sees, who sees the Lord Supreme, existing equally in all beings, deathless in the dying."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [:]),
        Verse(chapter: 13, verse: "28",
              sanskrit: #"समं पश्यन्हि सर्वत्र समवस्थितमीश्वरम् ।\#nन हिनस्त्यात्मनाऽऽत्मानं ततो याति परां गतिम् ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna says that the person who sees the same Lord present equally everywhere does not harm the self by the self, and so reaches the highest goal. If the same Self is in all, harming another is harming oneself. The lesson is that seeing the divine in everyone naturally leads to kindness, because we recognise ourselves in others."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Since seeing the Lord equally existent everywhere, he injures not Self by self, and so goes to the highest Goal."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Seeing the Lord equally existent everywhere, he injures not Self by self."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 13, verse: "29",
              sanskrit: #"प्रकृत्यैव च कर्माणि क्रियमाणानि सर्वशः ।\#nयः पश्यति तथाऽऽत्मानमकर्तारं स पश्यति ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna says that the one who truly sees understands that all actions are carried out by nature, and that the Self is not the doer. Our bodies and minds act according to their nature, while our deepest self is the witness. The lesson is that recognising this frees us from the heavy pride and guilt of thinking 'I alone am doing everything'."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"He sees, who sees that all actions are done by Prakriti alone and that the Self is actionless."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [:]),
        Verse(chapter: 13, verse: "32",
              sanskrit: #"यथा सर्वगतं सौक्ष्म्यादाकाशं नोपलिप्यते ।\#nसर्वत्रावस्थितो देहे तथाऽऽत्मा नोपलिप्यते ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna compares the Self to space. Space is everywhere, yet because it is so subtle, nothing stains it. In the same way, the Self dwells in every body but is never tainted by what the body does or experiences. The lesson is that our deepest nature remains pure and untouched, whatever passes through our lives."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"As the all-pervading Akâsha, because of its subtlety, is not tainted, so the Self existent in the body everywhere is not tainted."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"As the all-pervading Akâsha … is not tainted, so the Self … is not tainted."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 13, verse: "33",
              sanskrit: #"यथा प्रकाशयत्येकः कृत्स्नं लोकमिमं रविः ।\#nक्षेत्रं क्षेत्री तथा कृत्स्नं प्रकाशयति भारत ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna offers a simple image: just as one sun lights up the whole world, the one Self lights up the whole body and mind. It is the awareness by which everything in us is known. The lesson is that consciousness is like an inner sun, a single light that makes all our experience possible."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"As the one sun illumines all this world, so does He who abides in the Kshetra, O descendant of Bharata, illumine the whole Kshetra."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"As the one sun illumines all this world, so does He … illumine the whole Kshetra."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 14, verse: "17",
              sanskrit: #"सत्त्वात्सञ्जायते ज्ञानं रजसो लोभ एव च ।\#nप्रमादमोहौ तमसो भवतोऽज्ञानमेव च ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"In his teaching on the three qualities of nature, Krishna sums up their fruits: from clarity and goodness comes wisdom, from restless passion comes greed, and from dullness come confusion, delusion and ignorance. The lesson is to notice which quality is driving us at any moment, because each one leads somewhere very different."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"From Sattva arises wisdom, and greed from Rajas; miscomprehension, delusion and ignorance arise from Tamas."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"From Sattva arises wisdom, and greed from Rajas."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 14, verse: "20",
              sanskrit: #"गुणानेतानतीत्य त्रीन्देही देहसमुद्भवान् ।\#nजन्ममृत्युजरादुःखैर्विमुक्तोऽमृतमश्नुते ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna says that the person who goes beyond the three qualities of nature, from which the body itself arises, is freed from birth, death, old age and sorrow, and attains the deathless. Even goodness, the highest of the three, must finally be transcended. The lesson is that true freedom lies beyond all the moods and qualities of our nature, in the Self that is untouched by them."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"The embodied one having gone beyond these three Gunas out of which the body is evolved, is freed from birth, death, decay and pain, and attains to immortality."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"The embodied one having gone beyond these three Gunas … attains to immortality."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 15, verse: "5",
              sanskrit: #"निर्मानमोहा जितसङ्गदोषा अध्यात्मनित्या विनिवृत्तकामाः ।\#nद्वन्द्वैर्विमुक्ताः सुखदुःखसंज्ञै र्गच्छन्त्यमूढाः पदमव्ययं तत् ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna describes who reaches the eternal goal: those free from pride and delusion, who have overcome the fault of attachment, who live always in the Self, whose desires have fallen away, and who are free from the pull of pleasure and pain. The lesson is that the path to the highest is a gradual clearing away of pride, attachment and craving."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Free from pride and delusion, with the evil of attachment conquered, ever dwelling in the Self, with desires completely receded, liberated from the pairs of opposites known as pleasure and pain, the undeluded reach that Goal Eternal."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Free from pride and delusion … the undeluded reach that Goal Eternal."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 15, verse: "6",
              sanskrit: #"न तद्भासयते सूर्यो न शशाङ्को न पावकः ।\#nयद्गत्वा न निवर्तन्ते तद्धाम परमं मम ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna describes his supreme abode: it is not lit by the sun, nor the moon, nor fire, and those who reach it do not return. It is a light that needs no other light. The lesson is that the ultimate reality is self-luminous, the source of light rather than something lit by it, and reaching it is a final homecoming."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"That the sun illumines not, nor the moon, nor fire; that is My Supreme Abode, going whither they return not."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"That the sun illumines not, nor the moon, nor fire; that is My Supreme Abode."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 15, verse: "7",
              sanskrit: #"ममैवांशो जीवलोके जीवभूतः सनातनः ।\#nमनःषष्ठानीन्द्रियाणि प्रकृतिस्थानि कर्षति ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna says that the living soul in every being is an eternal part of himself. Clothed in nature, it draws to itself the senses and the mind. Each of us, then, is a fragment of the divine living in the world. The lesson is that our inner essence shares in something eternal and sacred, which gives every life its dignity."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"An eternal portion of Myself having become a living soul in the world of life, draws (to itself) the (five) senses with mind for the sixth, abiding in Prakriti."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"An eternal portion of Myself … draws (to itself) the (five) senses with mind for the sixth."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 15, verse: "15",
              sanskrit: #"सर्वस्य चाहं हृदि सन्निविष्टो मत्तः स्मृतिर्ज्ञानमपोहनं च ।\#nवेदैश्च सर्वैरहमेव वेद्यो वेदान्तकृद्वेदविदेव चाहम् ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna says he dwells in the hearts of all, and that memory, knowledge and even their loss come from him. He is what all scriptures seek to know, the author of their deepest teaching, and the knower of them. The lesson is that the divine is not only out in the world but within us, at the very source of our thinking and understanding."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"I am centred in the hearts of all; memory and perception as well as their loss come from Me. I am verily that which has to be known by all the Vedas, I indeed am the Author of the Vedânta, and the Knower of the Veda am I."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"I am centred in the hearts of all; memory and perception as well as their loss come from Me."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 16, verse: "3",
              sanskrit: #"तेजः क्षमा धृतिः शौचमद्रोहो नातिमानिता ।\#nभवन्ति सम्पदं दैवीमभिजातस्य भारत ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna completes his list of the divine qualities: vigour, forgiveness, fortitude, purity, freedom from hatred, and freedom from pride. These, he says, belong to someone born for a divine nature. The lesson is that spiritual greatness shows itself in very human virtues, such as forgiving others, enduring hardship and staying humble."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Boldness, forgiveness, fortitude, purity, absence of hatred, absence of pride; these belong to one born for a divine state, O descendant of Bharata."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Forgiveness, fortitude … absence of hatred … these belong to one born for a divine state."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 16, verse: "21",
              sanskrit: #"त्रिविधं नरकस्येदं द्वारं नाशनमात्मनः ।\#nकामः क्रोधस्तथा लोभस्तस्मादेतत्त्रयं त्यजेत् ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Chapter 16 contrasts divine qualities, like fearlessness, honesty and compassion, with demonic ones, like arrogance and cruelty. Krishna sums up the danger in one verse: desire, anger and greed are the three gates to hell, the self's destruction. He urges that all three be given up. The lesson is to know which inner forces pull you towards harm, and to guard those gates carefully."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Triple is this gate of hell, destructive of the self,—lust, anger and greed; therefore one should forsake these three."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Triple is this gate of hell … lust, anger and greed; therefore one should forsake these three."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 16, verse: "22",
              sanskrit: #"एतैर्विमुक्तः कौन्तेय तमोद्वारैस्त्रिभिर्नरः ।\#nआचरत्यात्मनः श्रेयस्ततो याति परां गतिम् ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Having named lust, anger and greed as the three gates to darkness, Krishna says that whoever is freed from them does what is truly good for themselves and reaches the highest goal. Escaping these three is not a loss but a liberation. The lesson is that overcoming craving, anger and greed is the best thing we can do for our own wellbeing."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"The man who has got beyond these three gates of darkness, O son of Kunti, practises what is good for himself, and thus goes to the Goal Supreme."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"The man who has got beyond these three gates of darkness … goes to the Goal Supreme."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 17, verse: "3",
              sanskrit: #"सत्त्वानुरूपा सर्वस्य श्रद्धा भवति भारत ।\#nश्रद्धामयोऽयं पुरुषो यो यच्छ्रद्धः स एव सः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"In Chapter 17, Arjuna asks about people who worship with faith but ignore the scriptures. Krishna explains that faith comes in three kinds, shaped by a person's nature. Then he makes a profound statement: a person is made of their faith; whatever their faith is, that is what they are. The lesson is that what we deeply believe and trust shapes who we become, so it is worth choosing it wisely."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"The Shraddhâ of each is according to his natural disposition, O descendant of Bharata. The man consists of his Shraddhâ; he verily is what his Shraddhâ is."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"The man consists of his Shraddhâ; he verily is what his Shraddhâ is."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 17, verse: "15",
              sanskrit: #"अनुद्वेगकरं वाक्यं सत्यं प्रियहितं च यत् ।\#nस्वाध्यायाभ्यसनं चैव वाङ्मयं तप उच्यते ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"In Chapter 17, Krishna describes austerity, meaning self-discipline, of the body, the speech and the mind. Discipline of speech means words that cause no distress, that are true, pleasant and beneficial, along with regular study of sacred texts. It is a practical guide for everyday conversation. The lesson is that how we speak is itself a spiritual practice: speak truthfully, kindly and helpfully."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Speech which causes no vexation, and is true, as also agreeable and beneficial, and regular study of the Vedas,—these are said to form the austerity of speech."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Speech which causes no vexation, and is true … the austerity of speech."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 17, verse: "16",
              sanskrit: #"मनःप्रसादः सौम्यत्वं मौनमात्मविनिग्रहः ।\#nभावसंशुद्धिरित्येतत्तपो मानसमुच्यते ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna describes three kinds of discipline: of body, speech and mind. This verse gives the discipline of the mind: serenity, kindness, silence, self-control and purity of motive. None of these require special rituals; they are inner habits. The lesson is that the most important practice is cultivating a calm, kind and honest mind."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Serenity of mind, kindliness, silence, self-control, honesty of motive,—this is called the mental austerity."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Serenity of mind, kindliness, silence, self-control, honesty of motive."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 17, verse: "20",
              sanskrit: #"दातव्यमिति यद्दानं दीयतेऽनुपकारिणे ।\#nदेशे काले च पात्रे च तद्दानं सात्त्विकं स्मृतम् ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna describes the purest kind of giving: a gift given simply because giving is right, to someone who cannot repay it, at the right place and time, and to a worthy person. There is no expectation of return. The lesson is that true generosity asks for nothing back, and is offered thoughtfully as well as freely."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"To give is right, gift given with this idea, to one who does no service in return, in a fit place and to a worthy person, that gift is held to be Sâttvika."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"To give is right … to one who does no service in return … that gift is held to be Sâttvika."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 18, verse: "11",
              sanskrit: #"न हि देहभृता शक्यं त्यक्तुं कर्माण्यशेषतः ।\#nयस्तु कर्मफलत्यागी स त्यागीत्यभिधीयते ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"In the final chapter, Krishna clarifies what renunciation really means. No embodied being can give up action entirely, so the true renunciate is the one who gives up the fruits of action. The lesson is that we cannot stop acting, but we can stop clinging to results, and that is real renunciation."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Actions cannot be entirely relinquished by an embodied being, but he who relinquishes the fruits of action is called a relinquisher."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"He who relinquishes the fruits of action is called a relinquisher."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 18, verse: "37",
              sanskrit: #"यत्तदग्रे विषमिव परिणामेऽमृतोपमम् ।\#nतत्सुखं सात्त्विकं प्रोक्तमात्मबुद्धिप्रसादजम् ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna describes the highest kind of happiness: it feels like poison at first but becomes like nectar in the end, and it arises from a clear understanding of the Self. Discipline, study and self-control can be hard at first, yet they lead to lasting joy. The lesson is that the most valuable happiness often requires patience and effort before it ripens."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"That which is like poison at first, but like nectar at the end; that happiness is declared to be Sâttvika, born of the translucence of intellect due to Self-realisation."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"That which is like poison at first, but like nectar at the end … is declared to be Sâttvika."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 18, verse: "38",
              sanskrit: #"विषयेन्द्रियसंयोगाद्यत्तदग्रेऽमृतोपमम् ।\#nपरिणामे विषमिव तत्सुखं राजसं स्मृतम् ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"In contrast to the previous verse, Krishna describes the happiness that comes from the contact of the senses with their objects: it feels like nectar at first but becomes like poison in the end. Quick pleasures often leave us worse off. The lesson is to look beyond how something feels in the moment and ask where it will lead."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"That which arises from the contact of object with sense, at first like nectar, but at the end like poison, that happiness is declared to be Râjasika."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"At first like nectar, but at the end like poison, that happiness is declared to be Râjasika."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 18, verse: "45",
              sanskrit: #"स्वे स्वे कर्मण्यभिरतः संसिद्धिं लभते नरः ।\#nस्वकर्मनिरतः सिद्धिं यथा विन्दति तच्छृणु ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna says that each person, devoted to their own work, attains perfection, and he goes on to explain how. Spiritual fulfilment is not only for monks and scholars; it can be reached through whatever work is truly ours. The lesson is that doing our own work wholeheartedly and well can itself be a path to perfection."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Devoted each to his own duty, man attains the highest perfection. How engaged in his own duty, he attains perfection, that hear."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Devoted each to his own duty, man attains the highest perfection."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 18, verse: "48",
              sanskrit: #"सहजं कर्म कौन्तेय सदोषमपि न त्यजेत् ।\#nसर्वारम्भा हि दोषेण धूमेनाग्निरिवावृताः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Chapter 18 is Krishna's grand summary of the whole teaching. Here he returns to Arjuna's original crisis: should he abandon his duty as a warrior because it involves harm? Krishna says no one should abandon the work that is natural to them, even if it has flaws, because every undertaking is clouded by some fault, just as fire is surrounded by smoke. The lesson is not to wait for a perfect path; do your own work sincerely, accepting that nothing in life is flawless."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"One should not relinquish, O son of Kunti, the duty to which one is born, though it is attended with evil; for, all undertakings are enveloped by evil, as fire by smoke."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"One should not relinquish … the duty to which one is born, though it is attended with evil."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 18, verse: "54",
              sanskrit: #"ब्रह्मभूतः प्रसन्नात्मा न शोचति न काङ्क्षति ।\#nसमः सर्वेषु भूतेषु मद्भक्तिं लभते पराम् ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna describes someone who has become one with the highest: serene, neither grieving nor craving, and equal towards all beings. Such a person, he says, attains supreme devotion to him. Knowledge and love meet at the summit. The lesson is that the deepest realisation does not make us cold; it opens into wholehearted devotion."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Brahman-become, tranquil-minded, he neither grieves nor desires; the same to all beings, he attains to supreme devotion unto Me."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Brahman-become, tranquil-minded, he neither grieves nor desires."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 18, verse: "58",
              sanskrit: #"मच्चित्तः सर्वदुर्गाणि मत्प्रसादात्तरिष्यसि ।\#nअथ चेत्त्वमहङ्कारान्न श्रोष्यसि विनङ्क्ष्यसि ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna tells Arjuna that if he keeps his mind on him, he will overcome every obstacle by his grace, but if, out of pride, he will not listen, he will be lost. It is both a promise and a warning. The lesson is that humility and trust carry us through difficulties, while stubborn pride shuts out the help we need."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Fixing thy mind on Me, thou shalt, by My grace, overcome all obstacles; but if from self-conceit thou wilt not hear Me, thou shalt perish."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Fixing thy mind on Me, thou shalt, by My grace, overcome all obstacles."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 18, verse: "61",
              sanskrit: #"ईश्वरः सर्वभूतानां हृद्देशेऽर्जुन तिष्ठति ।\#nभ्रामयन्सर्वभूतानि यन्त्रारूढानि मायया ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna says that the Lord dwells in the heart of every being and, by his power, moves all beings as if they were mounted on a machine. Much of what we do is driven by forces we barely notice. The lesson is that recognising this inner presence helps us see how much is beyond our control, and invites us to turn towards it."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"The Lord, O Arjuna, dwells in the hearts of all beings, causing all beings, by His Mâyâ, to revolve, (as if) mounted on a machine."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"The Lord … dwells in the hearts of all beings."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 18, verse: "62",
              sanskrit: #"तमेव शरणं गच्छ सर्वभावेन भारत ।\#nतत्प्रसादात्परां शान्तिं स्थानं प्राप्स्यसि शाश्वतम् ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Following from the previous verse, Krishna urges Arjuna to take refuge in that inner Lord with his whole being. By that grace, he says, Arjuna will reach supreme peace and the eternal home. The lesson is that wholehearted trust in the divine within is a path to the deepest peace."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Take refuge in Him with all thy heart, O Bhârata; by His grace shalt thou attain supreme peace (and) the eternal abode."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Take refuge in Him with all thy heart … by His grace shalt thou attain supreme peace."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 18, verse: "63",
              sanskrit: #"इति ते ज्ञानमाख्यातं गुह्याद्गुह्यतरं मया ।\#nविमृश्यैतदशेषेण यथेच्छसि तथा कुरु ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Near the end of the Gita, after giving Arjuna the most profound wisdom, Krishna says: reflect on it fully, and then do as you choose. He does not force or command. The lesson is that the Gita respects our freedom: real wisdom is something we must think through and choose for ourselves."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Thus has wisdom more profound than all profundities, been declared to thee by Me; reflecting over it fully, act as thou likest."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Wisdom more profound than all profundities … reflecting over it fully, act as thou likest."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 18, verse: "65",
              sanskrit: #"मन्मना भव मद्भक्तो मद्याजी मां नमस्कुरु ।\#nमामेवैष्यसि सत्यं ते प्रतिजाने प्रियोऽसि मे ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"Krishna repeats, almost word for word, the instruction that ended chapter 9: fix your mind on me, be devoted to me, worship me, bow to me, and you will come to me. Then he adds a personal promise: he truly promises this, because Arjuna is dear to him. The lesson is that the path ends in love, and that we are dear to the divine."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Occupy thy mind with Me, be devoted to Me, sacrifice to Me, bow down to Me. Thou shalt reach Myself; truly do I promise unto thee, (for) thou art dear to Me."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Occupy thy mind with Me … truly do I promise unto thee, (for) thou art dear to Me."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 18, verse: "66",
              sanskrit: #"सर्वधर्मान्परित्यज्य मामेकं शरणं व्रज ।\#nअहं त्वां सर्वपापेभ्यो मोक्षयिष्यामि मा शुचः ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"This is often called the final and most secret teaching of the Gita, near the very end of the conversation. After all the paths of action, knowledge, meditation and devotion, Krishna tells Arjuna to set aside every other concern, take refuge in him alone, and not grieve, because he will free him from all wrongs. Soon after, Arjuna declares that his confusion is gone and he is ready to act. The lesson is surrender and trust: after doing our best, we can let go of fear and rest in something greater."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Relinquishing all Dharmas take refuge in Me alone; I will liberate thee from all sins; grieve not."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Relinquishing all Dharmas take refuge in Me alone … grieve not."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 18, verse: "73",
              sanskrit: #"नष्टो मोहः स्मृतिर्लब्धा त्वत्प्रसादान्मयाच्युत ।\#nस्थितोऽस्मि गतसन्देहः करिष्ये वचनं तव ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"After the whole conversation, Arjuna speaks again. His confusion is gone, he says; he has regained his clarity by Krishna's grace, his doubts have vanished, he stands firm, and he will do as Krishna says. The Gita began with Arjuna collapsing in despair; it ends with him ready to act. The lesson is that understanding can transform paralysis into clear, courageous action."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Destroyed is my delusion, and I have gained my memory through Thy grace, O Achyuta. I am firm; my doubts are gone. I will do Thy word."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Destroyed is my delusion … I am firm; my doubts are gone. I will do Thy word."#,
              ]),
        Verse(chapter: 18, verse: "78",
              sanskrit: #"यत्र योगेश्वरः कृष्णो यत्र पार्थो धनुर्धरः ।\#nतत्र श्रीर्विजयो भूतिर्ध्रुवा नीतिर्मतिर्मम ॥"#,
              explanations: [
                .en: #"These are the very last words of the Gita, spoken by Sanjaya. Wherever Krishna, the master of yoga, and Arjuna, the archer, are together, there will be good fortune, victory, prosperity and sound judgment. The lesson is that wisdom joined with committed action, the divine guide and the human doer working together, is what brings true success."#,
              ],
              texts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Wherever is Krishna, the Lord of Yoga, wherever is Pârtha, the wielder of the bow, there are prosperity, victory, expansion, and sound policy: such is my conviction."#,
              ],
              shortTexts: [
                .swarupananda: #"Wherever is Krishna … there are prosperity, victory, expansion, and sound policy."#,
              ]),
    ]
}
